            <div xml:id="ni_d13">

                <pb facs="hu_d13-1" n="1" xml:id="ni_d13-1"/>

                <head>NI–13. Memorandum of Conversation, by the Assistant Secretary of State for
                    Inter-American Affairs (Rubottom)<anchor n="1" xml:id="ni_d13-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1959-04-04">April 4, 1959</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>SUBJECT</label>
                    <item>NICARAGUA: Visit of General Somoza</item>
                </list>
                <list subtype="participants" type="description">
                    <label>PARTICIPANTS</label>
                    <item>ARA-Mr. Rubottom</item>
                    <item>General Anastasio Somoza – Director of the Nicaraguan National
                        Guard</item>
                </list>
                <p>In addition to the points made in the memorandum of conversation of March
                        30,<anchor n="2" xml:id="ni_d13-f2"/> in my discussions with General Somoza
                    I also talked to him along the following lines in a private conversation
                    following the luncheon at the Nicaraguan Embassy: <list>
                        <label>1.</label>
                        <item>The United States felt that Nicaragua simply had to keep itself
                            absolutely clean in its dealings with its neighbors in Central America
                            and elsewhere or it can encounter serious complications in the OAS and
                            in public opinion throughout the Americas.</item>
                        <label>2.</label>
                        <item>It would be desirable for Nicaragua to desist in its efforts to
                            purchase arms in the United States, since we did not want to call undue
                            attention to this subject at this particular time.</item>
                        <label>3.</label>
                        <item>It is unfortunate that the thing of which the Somoza brothers are the
                            most proud, their name, is a serious political liability; they should
                            recognize that public opinion throughout the Americas simply is unable
                            to accept the fact that Nicaragua is being governed with a moderate hand
                            and that the people have civil liberties.</item>
                    </list></p>
                <p>The General seemed to accept all this in a friendly way and he even acknowledged
                    the veracity of the points made.</p>
            </div>
